# Package

Validate the config, then ship a release server or an ad-hoc macOS
`.app`. Field tables stay in
[Configuration](/docs/reference/configuration/). Rocci is **development**
`main`. Production signing, notarization, and OS installers are not
shipped.

:figure[caption: "Validate, then ship a release server or an ad-hoc macOS .app."] {{
    :img[src: "/assets/diagrams/delivery.svg", alt: "rocci validate checks rocci.toml. rocci build --release writes a server process plus assets for a reverse proxy. rocci bundle writes an ad-hoc macOS .app. There is no production signing.", loading: "lazy", decoding: "async"]
}}

## Configure

Unknown `rocci.toml` fields are errors. Validate before you change
anything:

```sh
rocci validate
```

`[http].host` is loopback only (`127.0.0.1` / `localhost`). Origins and
CSP belong in the configuration reference.

## Build

`rocci build --release` compiles a **cross-platform Roc binary** (the
application server) plus `assets/`. It is not a macOS `.app`.

```sh
rocci build --release examples/rocci/standalone/counter
rocci build --release examples/rocci/standalone/counter --target x64musl
```

`--target` requires `--release`. Run the artifact behind your own reverse
proxy; do not expose the preview window as production.

Container sketches live under `docker/` (`rocci-ops serve app`). They
consume the release tree; they do not invent a new install channel.

## Desktop application

Ad-hoc macOS `.app` bundling wraps the same app in a desktop window:

```sh
rocci bundle --config examples/rocci/standalone/counter/rocci.toml
```

Host-native only. `--target` is rejected for `.app`. The bundle is ad-hoc
signed. Standalone `.rocci` versus a directory with `main.roc` is the
same packaging choice as [custom](/docs/applications/custom/).
